          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Desmond Tutu</strong><br>\nDesmond Tutu was born on October 7, 1931, in Klerksdorp, South Africa. He grew up in a segregated country where apartheid, the policy of racial separation and discrimination, was law. Despite the challenges of apartheid, Tutu was inspired by his faith and the teachings of Jesus Christ to fight for equality. He decided to become a teacher, like his father, but later left teaching due to the enforcement of apartheid in education.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tutu's Religious Path</strong><br>\nDesmond Tutu studied theology after leaving teaching and was ordained as an Anglican priest in 1961. His religious career took him to England, where he earned a master's degree in theology from King's College London. As a priest, Tutu used his platform to speak out against the injustices of the apartheid system, combining his religious vocation with activism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Anti-Apartheid Activism</strong><br>\nAs an outspoken critic of apartheid, Desmond Tutu became a prominent leader in the South African Council of Churches. He advocated for nonviolent protest and international pressure to end apartheid. Tutu emphasized the common humanity of all races, earning him both respect and hostility in a divided nation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
